.\" Man page for cssgrep. Keep the OPTIONS section in sync with the USAGE
.\" string in cli.js.
.TH CSSGREP 1 "2026-07-12" "cssgrep 1.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cssgrep \- search HTML by CSS selector, grep-style
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cssgrep
.I selector
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B cssgrep
.I selector
.B \-r
.IR dir ...
.br
.B cssgrep
.B \-e
.RI \(aq[ label =] sel \(aq
.RB [ \-e
\&...]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B cat
.I file.html
| \fBcssgrep\fR \fIselector\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cssgrep
matches a CSS
.I selector
against parsed HTML and prints each hit grep-style. Unlike most HTML query
tools it tracks the source byte offset of every matched node, so
.B \-n
reports an accurate
.IR line : col
even on minified, single-line HTML, and the output plugs straight into
grep-aware editors (vim's
.BR grepprg ).
.PP
Input is read from the
.I file
arguments, recursively from directories with
.BR \-r ,
or from standard input when no path is given. Binary input, including standard
input (a NUL byte or a high ratio of control bytes in the first 8 KB), is
skipped with a note on standard error, suppressible with
.B \-s
or
.BR \-q .
As with
.BR grep ,
each matching line is printed on its own, once \(em however many matches sit
on it. With
.B \-n
there is one record per
.IR match ,
each with its own locator. A
.RI " file :"
prefix is added when searching multiple files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-selector " [\fIlabel\fR=]\fIsel\fR"
Add a selector (repeatable). Matches from all
.B \-e
selectors merge into one document-order stream, each tagged
.RI [ label ]
in the output (and as a
.I label
field with
.BR \-\-json ).
An unlabeled selector is tagged with its own text. A label is an identifier
before a
.BR = ;
a
.B =
outside brackets is never valid CSS, so attribute selectors like
.I [href=x]
are never mistaken for labels.
.B \-m
and
.B \-M
cap the merged stream, and print modes apply to every selector alike. With
.BR \-e ,
every positional argument is a file path, as with
.BR "grep \-e" .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Recurse into directory arguments (defaults to the current directory if none
are given).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-depth " n"
Limit
.B \-r
recursion depth.
.I 1
means the given directory only (no subdirectories).
.TP
.BI \-\-ext " list"
Comma-separated extensions to scan with
.B \-r
(default:
.IR html,htm ).
.TP
.BI \-\-include " glob"
Only search files whose name/path matches
.I glob
while recursing (repeatable). Replaces
.BR \-\-ext ;
the two cannot be combined.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore " \fIglob\fR"
Skip files/dirs matching
.I glob
while recursing (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " glob"
Alias for
.BR \-\-ignore .
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore\-file " path"
Read ignore globs from
.IR path ,
one per line
.RB ( #
comments and blank lines ignored), like a
.IR .gitignore .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-follow
Follow symbolic links while recursing with
.B \-r
(they are skipped by default). Each physical directory is visited once, so
symlink cycles are safe.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-line\-number
Prefix each match with its
.IR line : col
locator. The column counts bytes (what vim's
.B grepformat
.I %c
expects), pointing at the opening
.B <
of the matched tag. Excludes
.B \-c
and
.BR \-p .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-print
Pretty-print the matched node's HTML, re-indented from scratch. No
.IR line : col
locator is shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-attr " name"
Print the value of attribute
.I name
for each match (nodes without it are skipped; if every match is skipped the
exit status is 1). The name is matched case-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-\-text
Print the matched node's text content, whitespace collapsed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object per match (NDJSON) with
.IR file ", " line ", " col ", " attribs ", " html ", " text
(plus
.I label
with
.BR \-e ).
.I attribs
is the element's attribute object, names lowercased by the parser.
.TP
.BI \-\-parent " n"
Report the
.IR n -th
element ancestor of each match instead of the match itself (de-duplicated).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-max\-width " \fIn\fR"
Truncate the shown line to
.I n
columns (adds an ellipsis).
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-after\-context " \fIn\fR"
Print
.I n
source lines after each match.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-before\-context " \fIn\fR"
Print
.I n
source lines before each match.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-context " \fIn\fR"
Print
.I n
source lines before and after each match.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-count " \fIn\fR"
Stop after
.I n
matches per file.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-max\-total " \fIn\fR"
Stop after
.I n
matches in total across all files. The budget counts matches, not files;
combined with
.B \-l
or
.B \-L
scanning stops once the budget is spent, which can cut the file list short.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count
Print only a count of matches (per file when relevant). Files with no match
report a zero count, as in
.BR grep .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-files\-with\-matches
Print only the names of files that have a match.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-files\-without\-match
Print only the names of files with no match.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing; exit 0 on the first match, 1 if none.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-no\-messages
Suppress error messages for unreadable or missing files.
.TP
.BR \-0 ", " \-\-null
Separate the file name with a NUL byte (for
.BR "xargs \-0" ).
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-with\-filename
Always print the file name prefix, even for a single file or standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-filename
Never print the file name prefix, even when searching multiple files.
.TP
.BR \-\-color [ =\fIwhen\fR ]
Colorize output:
.I auto
(default),
.IR always ", or " never .
A bare
.B \-\-color
means
.IR auto ,
as in
.BR grep ;
use
.B \-\-color=always
to force color into a pipe.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.PP
Boolean short flags combine
.RB ( \-rn
is
.BR "\-r \-n" ),
and a value-taking flag may close such a cluster
.RB ( \-rnw100 ).
Long options take a value with
.B =
or as the next word
.RB ( \-\-max\-width=100 ", " "\-\-ext htm" ).
.PP
Globs for
.BR \-\-include ", " \-\-ignore ", and " \-\-exclude
support
.BR * " (within a path segment), " ** " (across " / ),
.BR ? " (one non-slash char), and brace alternation like " *.{html,htm} .
.SH EMBEDDED HTML
Files with JS/TS extensions
.RB ( .js ", " .mjs ", " .cjs ", " .jsx ", " .ts ", " .mts ", " .cts ", " .tsx )
are searched for HTML inside template literals: every backtick literal whose
content looks like markup is parsed as its own document, and each
.RI \(dq${ expr }\(dq
interpolation is masked to same-length whitespace, so match locators point
directly into the host file. Printed lines and
.BR \-\-json 's
.I html
field show the original source with interpolations visible; selector
matching sees holes as whitespace (so
.I class=\(dq${cls} card\(dq
matches
.IR .card ).
Fragments parse independently \(em an unclosed tag in one literal never
swallows the next. With
.BR \-r ,
add the extensions via
.BR "\-\-ext js,ts" .
JSX is not extracted (it is not HTML), and the rewrite operations treat these
files as plain HTML.
.SH WATCH MODE
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Keep running and repeat the search whenever a watched file changes. Requires
file or directory arguments (standard input cannot be watched); cannot be
combined with
.B \-q
or the rewrite operations. Runs until interrupted; Ctrl-C exits with
status 0, like
.BR watch (1).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clear
With
.B \-\-watch
on a terminal: append run results instead of clearing the screen.
.PP
Output adapts to its destination, like
.BR \-\-color=auto :
on a terminal each run clears the screen and reprints; piped output contains
no escape codes and appends each run after a
.RI \(dq== " HH:MM:SS changed-file " ==\(dq
separator; with
.B \-\-json
each rerun emits an NDJSON
.I {"event":"run","changed":...,"matches":n}
record followed by the match records.
.PP
Every rerun repeats the full directory walk, so new files are discovered (and
deleted ones dropped) under the same
.BR \-\-include / \-\-ignore / \-\-ext
rules as a fresh invocation; change bursts are debounced. Watching uses the
operating system's native file events (no polling), which may be unreliable
on network or virtual filesystems.
.SH REWRITING
Five repeatable, freely combinable operations edit the matched elements
instead of reporting them:
.TP
.BI \-\-add\-class " c"
Add a class to each matched element (deduplicated; attribute created if
missing).
.TP
.BI \-\-remove\-class " c"
Remove a class; the attribute is dropped when emptied.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-attr " k=v"
Set attribute
.I k
to
.IR v ,
adding it if missing. The value is escaped.
.TP
.BI \-\-remove\-attr " k"
Remove attribute
.I k
(all source occurrences).
.TP
.BI \-\-rename\-tag " t"
Rename the element \(em its closing tag too, when one explicitly exists
(voids like
.IR <img> ,
self-closing and implied closes are left without one).
.TP
.B \-\-diff
Emit a unified diff instead of the rewritten document. Required for multiple
files; apply with
.BR git-apply (1)
or
.BR patch (1).
.PP
Only the matched tags' bytes change: matching runs once against the original
document and edits are byte-splices, so quoting, entities and formatting
everywhere else pass through untouched. Operations compose in a fixed order
\(em rename \(-> remove-attr \(-> set-attr \(-> remove-class \(-> add-class
\(em regardless of argument order.
.B \-\-parent
composes with rewriting. A single input prints the rewritten document to
standard output;
.B cssgrep
never writes a file. Input that is not valid UTF-8 is refused (exit 2).
Rewriting cannot be combined with
.BR \-n ", " \-p ", " \-\-attr ", " \-\-text ", " \-\-json ", " \-c ", "
.BR \-l ", " \-L ", " \-q ", " \-A / \-B / \-C ", " \-m / \-M ", " \-w ", "
.BR \-0 " or " \-e .
Exit status: 0 if anything was edited, 1 if nothing matched, 2 on error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
One or more matches were found (or, when rewriting, edits were made).
.TP
.B 1
No matches were found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (bad option, unreadable file, invalid selector).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Find every link in a page:
.B cssgrep a page.html
.TP
Locate inputs with a locator, even in minified HTML:
.B cssgrep \-n "input[name]" min.html
.TP
Recurse a tree, skipping vendored files:
.B cssgrep \-rn .error \-i node_modules src/
.TP
Extract attribute values for scripting:
.B cssgrep \-\-attr href "a.external" page.html
.TP
Scrape several labeled fields in one pass:
.B cssgrep \-e "title=h1" \-e "price=.card .price" \-\-json page.html
.TP
Rename every <b> to <strong> across a tree, review, then apply:
.B cssgrep b \-\-rename\-tag strong \-r src/ \-\-diff | git apply
.SH INVERTING MATCHES
There is no
.B \-v
flag: CSS expresses inversion in the selector itself via
.BR :not() " (complex selectors and selector lists included) and " :has() ,
scoped to the elements of interest \(em a flag would have to invent its own
answer to \(lqwhich non-matching elements?\(rq.
.TP
Images missing alt text:
.B cssgrep \(dqimg:not([alt])\(dq \-rn .
.TP
Cards containing no link:
.B cssgrep \(dqdiv.card:not(:has(a))\(dq page.html
.TP
Links outside nav and footer:
.B cssgrep \(dqa:not(nav a, footer a)\(dq page.html
.PP
For whole files without any match, use
.BR \-L .
.SH VIM INTEGRATION
Wire
.B cssgrep
into vim's
.B :grep
by pointing
.B grepprg
at it and teaching
.B grepformat
the
.IR file : line : col
locator:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set grepprg=cssgrep\e \-n\e \-r
set grepformat=%f:%l:%c\e %m
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1)
.SH AUTHOR
matias
